Z||000000||FieroJoe||03-09-2001||05:32 PM||||I am getting quite upset at this electronics project I have been working on for too long now.  It is a timer circuit for my Fiero, and I am absolutly sure that I have everything hooked up correctly.  But does it work?  NO!  Do the relays fire when they are supposed to?  NO!  Do they fire correctly and lock in the correct discharge circuit when fired manually?  YES!  So why dont they fire when they are supposed to???  AAAAHHHHHHHHHHH!  I need to get this done to do other things.<P>just gotta vent.  stupid stupid thing.  I am <B>not</B> going to let this stupid thing win!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||8
Z||000001||FieroJoe||03-09-2001||05:38 PM||||Might as well post the diagram so people know what I am talking about:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/timer.jpg"> <P>basic operation: when S1 is thrown (a SPST relay), the load is energized for a set amount of time, determined by the 50k pot.  Time on is supposed to be about 2 to 25 seconds if calculations are correct.<P>||||reg||11
Z||000002||theogre||03-09-2001||08:48 PM||||That's one way to do it I guess. would use a 555 in oneshot mode myself. more flexible. more stable in varying conditions. adds a few joints and a few cents to the cost.<P>you should probably concider shunt diodes on the coils to keep them from frying the transistor.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||overlord||03-09-2001||09:51 PM||||yarg!.<BR>dont do that man........im having visions of school all over again!.<P>I know a little bit about electronics, but never understood the whole  schematic thing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||03-09-2001||11:06 PM||||<B>theogre</B>:<P>I assume that the 555 you are talking about is some sort of IC or chip or something.  I havent gotten that far yet, learning wise in electronics; this is the most complicated (and troublesome) circuit I have built so far.  Everything in this circuit is very low voltage and current.  The load, at its maximum, will only draw up to 35 mA.  I blew the trans out though, but through a short (I think) and I also broke off the base wire  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  But before I broke it, I got a chance to put it in my DVOM which has some terminals you can put a trans into, to determine the HFe numbers.  Well, the bag the trans came in said a minimum of 63, and when I put it in, readout gave just a 1.  So either way I need a new one.<P>There are two main parts to this circuit.  The charge circuit (S1 at rest), and the discharge circuit.  When there is 12v present, all it does is charge up the cap.  When S1 is thrown, it is supposed to connect the parallel resistances to the base, to allow collector to emitter flow so the a and b relays will fire.  When the a relay fires and is held there by the emitter current, it locks the - side of the cap to the parallel resistances, and the b relay provides the + voltage for the load.  When the b relay provides current to the load, it also fires relay c, which locks in the discharge circuit from the charging circuit, so the relays will not be perpetually held open.  Hopefully, all these events (firing of a, b, c relays, will happen before the S1 returns to its original position)<P>Timing is determined by pot.  If the pot and 10k Ohm resistor create a resistence more than 25k ohm, more current will flow to the base, through the 25k ohm, allowing more collector to emitter current to flow and draining the cap faster.  <P><BR>So, now that you know how it works, how would I add this 555 that you were talking about, and what exactly does it do?  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||03-10-2001||09:48 PM||||Alright, so I went to radio shack today for some other things, and I found a TLC555 lower power timer complete with a little diagram.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/555pinout.jpg"> <P>So, how do I adjust the time that there is output current?  I guess I understand a little how to wire it, but I dont really understand that much logic of it.  Like I said, I am just a beginner.<P>thanks, to whoever can help me out!||||reg||3
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||03-10-2001||09:55 PM||||a 555 is a timer IC.. very common, though I haven't used them yet.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cliff Pennock||03-11-2001||07:23 AM||||One pot is the duty cycle, both pots regulate frequency.<P>CHARGE TIME (OUTPUT HIGH): 0.693*(Ra + Rb)*Ct<P>DISCHARGE TIME (OUTPUT LOW): 0.693*(Rb)*Ct <P>PERIOD: 0.693*(Ra + 2*Rb)*Ct <P>FREQUENCY: 1.44/((Ra + 2*Rb)*Ct)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||03-11-2001||08:36 AM||||I was born 40 years too soon.In 1968 I built a capacitor discharge ignition system for my new Plymouth.It ran for years.That was my only foray into solid state electronics.So all I can say now is,HUH?||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||03-11-2001||02:12 PM||||Radish Rack also has a little booklet for a dollar or 2 that has a whole bunch of common 555 timer circuits in it and all the math that goes with them.<P>astable isn't the one you want. you want a monostable "one shot" circuit. Astable will oscilate forever at whatever frequency it's set for. monostable only cycles once per reset.||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroJoe||03-11-2001||09:08 PM||||Well, for now, I will have to get that booklet and see what there is inside that I can use.  I will use the 555 timer circuit as a backup in case I can't get my circuit to work.<P>I will be getting a new trans tomorrow, and I will try it again.  I really want to use the first one, because I know exactly how it works.  Id rather have that in my car because I would have more of a feeling of accomplishment with it.  But, if I have to go with the 555, oh well.  I'll learn about how they work before though, I have a book that probably has ICs in it.<P>Thanks for the help||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||03-11-2001||11:17 PM||||Is it possible that your capacitor is discharging to quickly to allow the transistor to activate the other relays?<P><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mark||03-12-2001||09:45 PM||||Joe,<P>With that 25K resistor, you are pulling less than 0.5 mA base current.  You probably not turning the transistor on hard enough.  Measure the voltage across the transistor (E to C).  It probably doesn't drop much when the switch is actuated.<P>Look here <A HREF="http://www.national.com/ds/LM/LM555.pdf" TARGET=_blank>http://www.national.com/ds/LM/LM555.pdf</A> <BR>for a complete data sheet for a National 555 timer chip.  This would be a much better way to go.||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroJoe||03-12-2001||09:58 PM||||Actually, I got it to work tonight!  Almost perfectly too.  I put in a new trans, and it didnt work when I threw the switch.  So alright, nothing new there.  I put +12 to the emitter and it fired the relays like it was supposed to, and actually stayed open for about 2 seconds.  Cool, so I added another cap in series, thinking I needed more collector current.  I then realized that caps act against each other in series, so I took it out, and removed the 68 ohm collector resistor.  It worked when I threw the switch!  yay!<P>So, I messed around with the timing (pot), and I still could only get about 2 seconds out of it after I let it cool down (soldering heat made more resistance and less time on).  So, I added 2 more 100mF caps in paralell.  Total of 300mF of capacitance, but then one of them blew up.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  Alright, so it was in backwards!  smelled like peanut butter too, very interesting.  So, fixed everything, took ogre's advice and put in diodes at the relay coils to protect the trans, and now I can get from about .25 to 6 seconds of time on. <P>So I was pretty happy that I finally got it to work.  If I need more time on, I'll go with a bigger cap or mess around with the resistances, but for now it is working fine for me and I am proud that I figured it out.<P>Thank you all for your help and thank you Oreif for the parts!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||10
Z||000014||FieroJoe||03-12-2001||10:01 PM||||here is a pic of the finished product, ready to be installed along with a few other electrical things:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/topside.jpg">  <P>Yeah, I know, my constuction techniques arent all that good, but this is the 3rd PC mount electronics project that I have done, and I have not gotten into etching and all.  I am just a beginner, learning as I go and adding some neat stuff to my car!<P>The LED is so I can tell if the circuit is working properly.  If for any reason, once the unit is installed, that the load does not time on like it should, I can check to see if that LED is on and then I will know whether the relays are firing or not.  I figured out the hard way that you need at least a 33 ohm resistor for that particular LED... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Oh well, I know a lot more about electronic components now!<p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 03-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||DJRice||03-13-2001||08:23 AM||||So are you going to tell us what the thing is for or what?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||5
Z||000016||Cheever2||03-13-2001||08:52 AM||||Maybe you can make me a box with a switch, and when you throw the switch a hand comes out and turns it off, then the hand goes back into the box.  ||||reg||6
Z||000017||FieroJoe||03-13-2001||10:11 AM||||What it is for is to run my varad el scanner that I will have in my windsheild (night rider effect)  Whenever the power door locks are activated, the timer circuit will run, keep the EL scanner on for the set amount of time.  Also, I will have another switch to turn the circuit off entirely (see "Under seat switch mounting" topic in GFC) so the scanner dosent run, and also one of those push buttons is to run the timer without having to lock/unlock the doors.<P>So, now my next project is installing the keyless entry/alarm system, and incorporate this circuit into that so when I disarm the alarm, the doors unlock, and the scanner flashes for 6 seconds or whatever I set it to.<P>I know it seems kinda wierd, but I like little gadgets like that, and you'll barely be able to see the scanner once it is under the windsheild unless it is either flashing or you are really looking for it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||DaRkLoRD||03-15-2001||01:33 AM||||In my electro-mechanical controls lab class last semester, we were working with timers, relays etc. on a large scale.. they were industrial controls.. I was always confused by the schematics, so I learned how to hook up each component by what it did, not how the symbol looked on a sheet. I got 100% in the course, and never blew a single fuse.. though I did get a few minor shocks.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>120v through my hand... FUN!<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 03-15-2001).]||||reg||10
